<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kurukh people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oraon people, Oraon, Oraons</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oraon, who call themselves Kurukh, are an Adivasi people of eastern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>. They speak Kurukh, a Dravidian language, and traditionally farmed the uplands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chota-nagpur-plateau">Chota Nagpur</a> plateau. Many adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> through Lutheran, Catholic and Anglican missions from the 19th century, and they are one of the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-tribes">Scheduled Tribes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kurukh-people.jpg" alt="Kurukh people" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rocky international · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurukh_dance_of_Oraons_.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
